CH5_AFTER_CONCLUSION = [
    ("h2", "Technical Contributions"),
    ("b", ["A session-management library whose shortest configuration is its safest: opaque tokens, fail-closed store handling and five-minute access tokens, with unsafe combinations refused at start-up.",
           "A refresh-rotation design whose store-operation ordering keeps concurrent tabs signed in while still detecting replay, together with a revocation marker that closes the races between logout and issuance, each pinned by regression tests against real Redis.",
           "DPoP sender-constraining in which the refresh token's binding is checked before it is consumed, so that a thief without the key cannot end the victim's session.",
           "Measured documentation of how each token strategy behaves under Redis restart, outage, data loss and rollback, including the unfavourable PASETO case.",
           "A browser client that keeps credentials out of script-readable storage and coordinates logout across tabs without sharing credentials.",
           "An evidence practice in which security claims name their tests, evidence is labelled by source and commit, and a reproducible benchmark refuses to time any configuration that fails a security preflight."]),
    ("h2", "Lessons Learned"),
    ("p", "The most consistent lesson of the project is that passing tests describe what was tested, not what is true. Several defects were found by exercising a code path directly, outside the happy path the existing tests followed, and each looked correct on reading. Ordering of store operations turned out to matter more than any cryptographic choice: the behaviour of refresh rotation, revocation and grace adoption depends on which write becomes visible first. Local results also proved unreliable as evidence on their own; a stale build, a single Node.js version, an absent Redis or, in this project's final run, a port collision each produced results that would have been misleading if reported without checking. Finally, honest comparison requires choosing baselines that provide the same guarantee: a stateless JWT is faster because it does less, and reporting it beside a revocable session without that qualification would misrepresent both."),
]

